{"id":3485,"date":"2021-08-08T05:40:47","date_gmt":"2021-08-08T12:40:47","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=3485"},"modified":"2023-08-21T08:35:47","modified_gmt":"2023-08-21T15:35:47","slug":"2-1-variables-part-1-data-types","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/2-1-variables-part-1-data-types\/","title":{"rendered":"2.1: Variables Part 1: Data Types"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22144347e3e\" name=\"6a22144347e3e\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Goals and Steps to Success<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<h2><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">By the end of this lesson, you should be able to:<\/span><\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Define what a variable is in programming<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Explain how variables can be used in a program<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Describe the benefits of using variables in your programs<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">List the different types of data in python programs<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Explain why we have different data types and provide examples of when to use the different data types<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2>Steps to Success<\/h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Why variables?<\/li>\n<li>What&#8217;s in a name? Variable Naming Best Practices<\/li>\n<li>Types of variables<\/li>\n<li>Practice Exercises with Variables<\/li>\n<li>Going in Squares Again<\/li>\n<li>Challenge: The Drone Slide<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22144348033\" name=\"6a22144348033\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Why Variables?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<h2><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Why Variables?<\/span><\/h2>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">If you remember from the last lesson we discussed the concept of abstraction. We said that abstraction was a way of making code short, simpler, easier to use, and easier to understand. Variables are a tool we can use to continue to add abstraction to our code and add an additional feature, reusability! To learn more about variables in Python, watch the video below:<\/span><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div style=\"width: 1200px;\" class=\"wp-video\"><video class=\"wp-video-shortcode\" id=\"video-3485-1\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" preload=\"metadata\" controls=\"controls\"><source type=\"video\/mp4\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/08\/Variables-Part-1-Codrone-Mini-Python.mp4?_=1\" \/><a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/08\/Variables-Part-1-Codrone-Mini-Python.mp4\">https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/08\/Variables-Part-1-Codrone-Mini-Python.mp4<\/a><\/video><\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]Now that you understand the basics of how variables work, play around with creating variables and changing their values. Try to figure out the answers to the following questions:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Are there any numbers, letters, or characters that are not allowed in variable names?<\/li>\n<li>Are there any rules that you discovered for variables name?<\/li>\n<li>Is there a maximum or minimum length that a variable name can be?<\/li>\n<li>If you were going to come up with rules for variable names, what would your rules be?<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221443480ee\" name=\"6a221443480ee\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>What\u2019s in a Name? Variable Naming Best practices<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Now that we know how to create variables, lets look at some of the rules we need to follow when it comes to variables.<\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div style=\"width: 1200px;\" class=\"wp-video\"><video class=\"wp-video-shortcode\" id=\"video-3485-2\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" preload=\"metadata\" controls=\"controls\"><source type=\"video\/mp4\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/08\/Variables-Style-and-Syntax-Python-Codrone-Mini.mp4?_=2\" \/><a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/08\/Variables-Style-and-Syntax-Python-Codrone-Mini.mp4\">https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/08\/Variables-Style-and-Syntax-Python-Codrone-Mini.mp4<\/a><\/video><\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221443481a1\" name=\"6a221443481a1\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Types of variables<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">So far, we learned to store whole number values like 3 and 2, but these are completely different types of information that we might want to store. For example, how would we store the text of a social media post or keep track of its number of likes? We can use variables! Python supports a number of variable types, but we will focus on four that we will encounter most frequently in our programs.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><b>Int: <\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">positive and negative whole numbers like 10,-3, or 142<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><b>Float: <\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">positive and negative decimal values like 3.14, -1.2, or 54.214<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><b>String: <\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">a group of letters and\/or numbers such as \u201cHello World\u201d<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><b>Boolean<\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">: Can be set to True or False values<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">There are a few important rules that you should know regarding data types of variables. First is that strings must be enclosed in single or double quotes \u2018\u2019 or \u201c\u201d. For these lessons we will use primarily double quotes.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Boolean values use what are called <\/span><b>reserved words<\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">. These are words in a programming language that serve a specific purpose and therefore cannot be used as the names for variables and functions. The two reserved words that Boolean data types use are <\/span><b>True <\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">and <\/span><b>False<\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">. They must have the first letter capitalized and the rest lowercase as shown in order to be considered a Boolean value. This also means that you cannot name a variable or procedure True or False:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">True = 2 # will result in an error<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Going back to our social media post, we might choose to save the number of likes as an integer data type variable while storing the text of the post as a string. We could even create a variable called hasImage which could store the Boolean data type telling us whether or not the post has an image attached. What other information might we store and as what data types?<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Now that we know a little about data types, let&#8217;s try creating some different types of variables. In the python console, enter the following:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">&gt;&gt;&gt;w = 4\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\r\n&gt;&gt;&gt;x = 3.2\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\r\n&gt;&gt;&gt;phrase = \"Hello\"\u00a0\u00a0\r\n&gt;&gt;&gt;is_flying = True<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Once you have entered these in the console, look at the variables pane on the right. Next to the name of each variable after the equals sign you should see in parenthesis what the data type is as shown in the image below:<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;3264&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214434826b\" name=\"6a2214434826b\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Practice Exercises with Variables<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Now let\u2019s try changing some of the variable values and see what happens. Enter the following in the console:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">&gt;&gt;&gt;x=3<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">What is the variable type that you see for x now? It was a float before but now it is an int!. Let\u2019s try seeing if we can change the string variable <\/span><i><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">phrase<\/span><\/i><span style=\"font-weight: 400\"> to something else:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">&gt;&gt;&gt;phrase=3.14<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">You should see that <\/span><i><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">phrase<\/span><\/i><span style=\"font-weight: 400\"> is now a float instead of a string. This is because we assigned it a new value that was a decimal.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Now that we have a better understanding of the different data types, try the following exercises on your own:<\/span><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Change the value of is_flying to False<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Create 4 variables, each representing one of the four data types we discussed<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Try to create a variable with a phone number as its value.\u00a0<\/span>\n<ol>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">What happens?<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">How would you fix this issue?<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Try to create a variable with an email as its value.\u00a0<\/span>\n<ol>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">What happens?<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">How would you fix this issue?<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">What data type do emails and phone numbers have to be stored as?<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">What data type should you store a date as?<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Create two variables <\/span><i><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">a <\/span><\/i><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">and <\/span><i><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">b. <\/span><\/i><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Enter the command a=b<\/span>\n<ol>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">What happens?<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">What happens when you change the value of a? b?<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22144348307\" name=\"6a22144348307\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Going in Squares Again<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Now that we have a better understanding of the purpose of variables in general, let\u2019s look at an example where their use makes a program that we have written simpler. To get set up, complete the following steps:<\/span><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Create a new directory called 3<em>_variables<\/em><\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Create a new file called 3<em>_1_square<\/em><\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Copy the code from where you rewrote the the square challenge with the go() command<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Here is an example of what the code could look like:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">from CoDrone_mini import Direction # Using the go() command so need to import Direction\r\nimport CoDrone_mini\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\ndrone.go(Direction.FORWARD,2,20) #first leg of square\r\ndrone.go(Direction.LEFT,2,20)\r\ndrone.go(Direction.BACKWARD,2,20)\r\ndrone.go(Direction.RIGHT,2,20) # last leg of square\r\ndrone.land()\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Now, we will add two variables called duration and power:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">duration = 2 # integer value for how long to fly in a direction\r\npower = 50 # integer value for the power setting of the drone<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">We will now substitute the variables for the parameters in our go() commands as shown below:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">drone.go(Direction.FORWARD,duration,power)\r\ndrone.go(Direction.LEFT,duration,power)\r\ndrone.go(Direction.BACKWARD,duration,power)\r\ndrone.go(Direction.RIGHT,duration,power)<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">You may be thinking, \u201cThat didn\u2019t really save me any time and my code is actually a little bit longer. So why would I use variables here?\u201d The answer is that since we are flying in a square, all we have to do is change the values of the duration and power variables to make any size square we want. This code is now easier to read and understand since we used names that describe what the variables are and the code is much more useful as it can create a wide variety of squares with minimal change.\u00a0<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22144348371\" name=\"6a22144348371\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge: The Drone Slide<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Now that you know how to create variables, let\u2019s use them in a program with CoDrone Mini! For this challenge, you need to have your drone fly a zig-zag pattern like the one shown below:<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;3268&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<b>Rules:\u00a0<\/b><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Name the file 3_1_challenge<\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">You must use at least 2 integer or float variables<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">The forward motions in the zig-zag must be the same length<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">The side to side motions in the zig-zag must be the same length<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 8 Goals and Steps to Success [vc_column_text] By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: Define what a variable is in programming Explain how variables&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/2-1-variables-part-1-data-types\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":4150,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":true,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":3473,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1737,1782,1848,1793,1826,1482,853,694,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2135,2125,2123,2122,2134,2117],"class_list":["post-3485","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-beginner","lesson-tag-codrone-mini","lesson-tag-fundamentals","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","lesson-tag-variables","module-level-2","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/3485","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":13,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/3485\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":6349,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/3485\/revisions\/6349"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/4150"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=3485"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=3485"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=3485"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}